(vector
 (map
  (:adr/id "adr-2609113100-nothing-remains-to-measure-before-the-promotion")
  (:adr/title "Nothing remains to measure before the promotion")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-11")
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609112800-presence-is-not-verification-and-the-digest-beside-it-was-never-checked" "adr-2609112600-counting-a-name-is-not-counting-uses-of-a-mechanism"))
  (:adr/body "
## The question this iteration asked

Every input to the v3 decision is measured. What had not been checked is
whether the decision can be ENACTED from what exists: is there an
end-to-end v3 identity, and can the frozen corpus be regenerated by a
command rather than by hand. Those are readiness facts, not decisions, so
they are measurable now.

## The corpus is regenerable, and it is a fixed point

osaho's frozen vectors are a copy of kotoba-lang's
lang/code-identity-vectors.edn, whose header names a regenerator. Both
halves of that were checked rather than read:

- the copy is in sync with its authority -- ten vectors, identical
  definition CIDs and canonical hex
- the regenerator exists, runs, and reproduces the committed corpus
  byte-for-byte: ten vectors and twenty-one conformance fixtures, thirty
  one files, no diff

So on cutover the corpus costs one command, not a hand edit.

    cd <kotoba-lang worktree>
    kbb -M -e (load-file scripts/gen_code_identity_fixtures.clj)
    git status --porcelain   # empty today

## The v3 path stopped at bytes

The unwrap test converts a payload and encodes it, and stops. The identity
is a CID, and no frozen vector had ever had its v3 CID computed. So the
question what will v3 produce had no answer on the record, and a cutover
would have had nothing to check its regenerated corpus against.

The CID step is not the trivial part it looks like. Payload v1 hashed
pr-str output and labelled it dag-cbor -- the differential test's header
records that as the mistake this line of work exists to avoid. So the
label was measured: kotoba.value.v1's own value-cid labels dag-cbor, and
that is honest, because the value model is a schema ABOVE dag-cbor rather
than a different wire codec. The bytes decode. The same labeller the v2
identity uses is the right one for v3.

## Ten identities, pinned

Computed for all ten frozen vectors under the conversion as it stands --
magnitude rule included -- and pinned in the unwrap test:

- ten distinct values, so the conversion collapses no two
- each different from its v2 CID, so the conversion did something
- each identical on recompute

Pinned for the reason the v2 ones are: a change to the conversion moves
them and is seen, rather than landing quietly and moving every identity in
the workspace on cutover.

    cd <osaho worktree>
    kbb -M:test -n kotoba.kir.payload-v3-unwrap-test

## Seen red, and the first attempt did not reach them

Reverting the i64 branch to unconditional int64 -- the pre-fix state --
fails eighteen assertions in the earlier tests and moves NO pinned CID.
The frozen vectors carry bare integers, so that branch barely touches
them. This is the same fact the byte-delta measurement found two
iterations ago, arriving from the other side.

Negating the f64 unwrap moves exactly one pinned CID, :kir-f64, and
leaves nine. The pin is sensitive to what the payloads actually contain,
and it localises: one branch broken, one vector moved.

A control that does not reach the thing it is meant to guard is not a
control, and it looks exactly like one that passed. Recorded so the next
break chosen for this test is one the fixtures exercise.

## Where readiness stands

    corpus regenerable by one command      measured, fixed point
    v3 bytes for every vector              in test/, measured
    v3 CID for every vector                in test/, pinned this iteration
    detection if a deployed DefCID moves   landed last (A) iteration
    the one non-mechanical decision        answered: magnitude

Nothing remains to measure before the io-ipld promotion. What remains is
the promotion, which is the v3 decision itself and is not taken here.

## Not claimed

io-ipld stays test-only. The conversion and the pinned identities live in
test/ and move to src/ only with the promotion.
")))
